# Driving the Minecraft Server

When the user asks for in-game actions (build something, op a player,
teleport, set time/weather, run a command), use this priority order.

## 1. Shared tool core — MCP and `/agent`

All in-world actions go through `agent/tools.py` (guardrailed RCON via
`docker exec mc-lan rcon-cli`). Two entry points use the same code:

- **Cursor**: `minecraft-builder` MCP (`mcp/minecraft_build_mcp.py`)
- **In-game**: `/agent <prompt>` → Paper plugin → host orchestrator → Ollama/cloud

Always prefer typed tools for in-world actions:

- Inspection: `server_status`, `preflight`, `list_players`,
  `get_player_position`, `get_player_dimension`
- Admin: `op_player`, `say`, `teleport_player`, `set_gamemode`, `set_time`,
 `set_weather`, `give_haste`, `give_item`, `backup_now`,
 `run_command` (escape hatch)
- Building: `fill_box`, `clear_box`, `build_platform`, `build_sphere`,
 `build_cylinder`, `build_spiral_staircase`
- **Templates (quality-first):** `list_templates`, `paste_template_at_player`,
 `create_template_from_selection` — FAWE schematics from
 `agent/templates/catalog.json` + `agent/templates/schematics/` (mounted
 into `mc-lan` at `.../schematics/neighborhood/`). Named structures
 (coop, castle, barn, well, bridge) should use `paste_template_at_player`
 unless the player gives explicit WxH / radius constraints.
- Player-relative builds (parametric fallback): `build_house_at_player`,
 `build_castle_at_player`, `build_bridge_at_player`,
 `build_wall_at_player`, `build_animal_pen_at_player`. As of
 `TOOLS_VERSION=2.8.0` these builders ship with real interiors:
 `build_house_at_player(height>=7)` lays a floor divider every 4 blocks
 and a 2-wide staircase against the back-left wall;
 `build_bridge_at_player` adds material-matched railings + support
 pillars by default (`add_rails=false` / `add_supports=false` to opt
 out); `build_castle_at_player` adds an interior staircase to the
 wall-walk plus a ladder up one tower. Don't manually splice extra
 stairs/rails on top.
- Mobs / world: `summon_entity` (raw `/summon`),
 `build_animal_pen_at_player` (fenced pen + summoned animals)
- FAWE: `fawe_as_player`, `fawe_sphere_at_player`, `mine_shaft_at_player`

### Guardrails (don't bypass without asking)

- `fill_box` / shape builders cap at **200,000** blocks
- generated commands cap at **20,000**
- Raw `run_command` is the escape hatch — prefer typed tools

### Bedrock vs Java names

Bedrock players show up via Floodgate with a leading `.`. When the user
says "lunar", confirm whether they mean `.BedrockPlayer` (Bedrock) or a
Java handle. The dot is part of the name in commands.

## 2. Shell wrappers — for terminal work

When working from the host shell, use the project scripts instead of
hand-writing `docker exec`:

- `./mc.sh status | list | logs | console | backup`
- `./mc.sh op <name>` / `./mc.sh deop <name>`
- `./op.sh <name> [name...]` — bulk op
- `./mc.sh say "<message>"`
- `./mc.sh gamemode <mode> <player>`
- `./mc.sh cmd "<rcon command>"` — escape hatch

## 3. Raw `docker exec` — last resort

Only fall back to `docker exec -i mc-lan rcon-cli "<cmd>"` if neither MCP
nor the scripts can express what's needed.

## Building patterns

- Rectangular volumes → `fill_box` (`mode: replace|keep|hollow|outline`)
- Spheres / domes → `build_sphere` (`hollow: true` for shells)
- Towers / wells → `build_cylinder`
- Stairs → `build_spiral_staircase`
- Chicken coop / pig pen / castle / cottage / well / bridge →
  `paste_template_at_player` first (catalog id or alias). The catalog
  ships **101 verified `.schem` files** (`agent/templates/schematics/`)
  bind-mounted at `mc-lan:/data/plugins/FastAsyncWorldEdit/schematics/
  neighborhood/` and at `mc-agent:/app/data/...`. If the schematic is
  somehow missing, the tool runs the catalog `fallback` parametric
  builder + any `follow_up` (e.g. summon chickens). Explicit sizes
  (`21x21`, `radius 12`) → parametric tools instead.
- Re-seeding schematics (e.g. after a builder change): use
  `python3 -m agent.templates.seed --player <op> --only <id> --all`. The
  pipeline deletes the existing `.schem` first because FAWE's
  `//schem save` is silently no-overwrite, and waits between
  `//pos1`/`//pos2`/`//copy`/`//schem save` because `agentexec`
  dispatches one FAWE command per server tick.
- Publish a build: player FAWE selection → `create_template_from_selection`,
  then commit `catalog.json` + `schematics/*.schem` for git-curated sharing.
- Spawn a mob at coords → `summon_entity(entity, x, y, z, count)`
- Selections, copy/paste, `//regen`, brushes, schematics →
  `fawe_as_player` so the operation runs with that player's selection
- For absolute coordinates from console, vanilla `/fill` and `/setblock`
  via `run_command` is fine

Before any large or destructive op (clearing, replacing, regen), restate
the coordinates and approximate block count back to the user.
